// The International System of Units (SI), as Blade units of measure.
//
// `import units.SI` brings every name below into scope as a unit, so
// `Float<newton>`, `Float<joule>` and friends type-check against each other
// through the ordinary unit algebra:
//
//     import units.SI
//     let m: Float<kilogram> = 2.0
//     let a: Float<meter * second^-2> = 9.81
//     let w: Float<newton> = m * a          // checks
//
// COHERENT UNITS ONLY. Blade units carry a DIMENSION, not a scale: a
// declaration is an equation between exponent vectors, and there is nowhere to
// put a numeric factor. `Unit kilometer = meter` would not mean "1000 metres",
// it would mean "kilometre IS metre" -- a silent alias that makes a wrong
// program type-check. So this module defines only the SI units whose defining
// equation has the factor 1: the seven base units and the coherent derived
// units built from them. Deliberately absent, and why:
//
//   * every prefixed unit (kilometre, milligram, megawatt, ...) -- factor 10^n;
//   * minute / hour / day / degree -- factors 60, 3600, 86400, pi/180;
//   * litre, tonne, bar, electronvolt, dalton, astronomical unit -- factors;
//   * degree Celsius -- a factor of 1 but an OFFSET of 273.15, which the unit
//     algebra cannot express at all (it is affine, not linear).
//
// Convert at the boundary with a plain multiplication and annotate the result:
// `let d: Float<meter> = km_value * 1000.0`.
module units.SI

// The seven base units
//
// Each is a base dimension: an independent axis of the exponent vector every
// other unit here is written against.

/// Length. SI base unit, symbol m.
Unit meter

/// Mass. SI base unit, symbol kg. Note the SI base unit of mass is the
/// KILOgram, not the gram, so the coherent derived units below (newton,
/// joule, ...) are written against `kilogram` directly.
Unit kilogram

/// Time. SI base unit, symbol s.
Unit second

/// Electric current. SI base unit, symbol A.
Unit ampere

/// Thermodynamic temperature. SI base unit, symbol K. Absolute: there is no
/// `celsius` here, because a Celsius reading differs from a kelvin reading by
/// an additive offset and the unit algebra multiplies.
Unit kelvin

/// Amount of substance. SI base unit, symbol mol.
Unit mole

/// Luminous intensity. SI base unit, symbol cd.
Unit candela

// Coherent derived units
//
// Every definition below is an identity among the base units with the factor
// 1, so annotating a value with the named unit and annotating it with the
// spelled-out product are the same type.

/// Frequency, symbol Hz = s^-1. Periodic phenomena, cycles per second.
Unit hertz = second^-1

/// Force, symbol N = kg*m/s^2.
Unit newton = kilogram * meter / second^2

/// Pressure and stress, symbol Pa = N/m^2 = kg/(m*s^2).
Unit pascal = newton / meter^2

/// Energy, work, and heat, symbol J = N*m = kg*m^2/s^2.
Unit joule = newton * meter

/// Power and radiant flux, symbol W = J/s = kg*m^2/s^3.
Unit watt = joule / second

/// Electric charge, symbol C = A*s.
Unit coulomb = ampere * second

/// Electric potential difference, symbol V = W/A = kg*m^2/(s^3*A).
Unit volt = watt / ampere

/// Capacitance, symbol F = C/V.
Unit farad = coulomb / volt

/// Electric resistance, symbol ohm = V/A.
Unit ohm = volt / ampere

/// Electric conductance, symbol S = A/V. The reciprocal of `ohm`.
Unit siemens = ampere / volt

/// Magnetic flux, symbol Wb = V*s.
Unit weber = volt * second

/// Magnetic flux density, symbol T = Wb/m^2.
Unit tesla = weber / meter^2

/// Inductance, symbol H = Wb/A.
Unit henry = weber / ampere

/// Catalytic activity, symbol kat = mol/s.
Unit katal = mole / second

// The two dimensionless derived units
//
// SI defines the radian as m/m and the steradian as m^2/m^2, both of which
// cancel: an angle in radians IS a pure number, which is exactly why sin/cos
// take one. Blade agrees -- `Float<radian>` and an unannotated `Float64` are
// the same type here, so `sin(theta)` accepts a `Float<radian>` and no cast is
// needed anywhere. The names are still worth having: they document intent at a
// declaration site, and they let `lumen` and `lux` be written the SI way.
//
// Do NOT reach for a base-unit `Unit radian` to get angle checking. It would
// buy a little safety at the cost of rejecting every transcendental call on an
// angle, which is most of what angles are for.

/// Plane angle, symbol rad = m/m. Dimensionless by SI definition.
Unit radian = meter / meter

/// Solid angle, symbol sr = m^2/m^2. Dimensionless by SI definition.
Unit steradian = meter^2 / meter^2

/// Luminous flux, symbol lm = cd*sr. Equal to `candela`, since `steradian`
/// cancels -- that is the SI definition, not an approximation.
Unit lumen = candela * steradian

/// Illuminance, symbol lx = lm/m^2.
Unit lux = lumen / meter^2

// Ionising radiation
//
// `becquerel`, `gray` and `sievert` are dimensionally equal to `hertz`,
// `gray` and each other respectively. SI keeps the separate names because
// they mark different QUANTITIES, and so does this module; the checker sees
// through to the exponents, so a becquerel and a hertz are interchangeable to
// it, as they are in SI.

/// Activity referred to a radionuclide, symbol Bq = s^-1.
Unit becquerel = second^-1

/// Absorbed dose of ionising radiation, symbol Gy = J/kg = m^2/s^2.
Unit gray = joule / kilogram

/// Dose equivalent, symbol Sv = J/kg. Dimensionally `gray`; the distinct name
/// marks the biological weighting, which is a factor applied by the user.
Unit sievert = joule / kilogram
